posts.plaintext:
Serilog provides a simple and elegant way to log structured events, making it easier to analyze and debug issues in your application. Its flexible configuration options and a wide range of output sinks make it easy to set up and integrate with other tools. Serilog is capabilities are not limited to just logging; it also provides a rich set of features, such as filtering, enriching, and transforming log events, which help to make your logs more valuable and insightful.

In this comprehensive guide, we will explore everything you need to know about Serilog, from the basics to advanced topics. We will guide you through setting up Serilog, enabling the most used sinks, defining custom output formats, and working with configuration files.

We will also delve into more advanced topics, such as enabling asynchronous sinks, sink to Elastic Search, and sink to Application Insights, and show you how to use the Log4J formatter.

Check out the Table of Contents if you are looking for something in specific.

And of course you have readied in my previous post about Serilog, right?

Set up Serilog in .NET 6 as a logging providerLet’s set up Serilog as Logging Provider in the native logging system in .NET so you can use the Microsoft ILogger interface.rmauro.devRicardo Mauro


💬 Table of Contents

 * Basic Set Up for .NET Application
 * Enabling Serilog Internal Logger / aka SelfLog
 * Defining Custom Output Formats
 * Configuring Output Templates
 * Working with JSON Configuration File
 * Working with XML Configuration File
 * Enabling Asynchronous Sink
 * Enabling Elastic Search Sink
 * Some Common Sinks
 * Making your own Enricher
 * Advanced - Write your own Custom Sink
 * More Resources about Serilog


Basic Set Up for .NET Application

Before we get started let's use an basic Web Project as our baseline.

Execute the following commands to add the required Serilog packages to our lab.

dotnet add package Serilog
dotnet add package Serilog.Extensions.Hosting
dotnet add package Serilog.Sinks.Console
dotnet add package Serilog.Sinks.Debug
dotnet add package Serilog.Sinks.File

Resulting in.

<ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog" Version="2.12.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Extensions.Hosting" Version="5.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.Console" Version="4.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.Debug" Version="2.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.File" Version="5.0.0" />
</ItemGroup>

With the Serilog packages installed the next step is to bootstrap the Serilog Logger with the Sinks we have installed and wire up with Microsoft ILogger interface.

Open the file Program.cs and make the following changes.

using Serilog;

//bootstrap Logger with Sinks and Minimum Level of Debug
Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .MinimumLevel.Debug()
    .WriteTo.Console()
    .WriteTo.Debug()
    .WriteTo.File("debug.log")
    .CreateLogger();

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

//wire up the ILogger<T> interface with Serilog
builder.Host.UseSerilog();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/", () => "Hello World!");

app.Run();

With this we're ready to start writing our application logic without worring where to write logs. The interface Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger<T>  is wire with Serilog and the Sinks are already configured.

💡Sink Console: Will write in the Console Window of the application
Sink File: Will write in a file called "debug.log"
Sink Debug: Will write into the Debug Window of Visual Studio


Enabling Serilog internal Error Log / aka SelfLob

Enabling Serilog internal error logs, also known as SelfLog, is an important step in debugging issues related to Serilog configuration or initialization. When you enable SelfLog, Serilog logs internal errors to its own internal logging system, which can be useful when diagnosing issues with your logging configuration.

To enable SelfLog, you need to call the Serilog.Debugging.SelfLog.Enable method in your code. The Enable method takes a delegate as its argument, which is called when Serilog encounters an internal error. You can use this delegate to log the error messages to a specific sink or location.

//writes into visual studio debug window
Serilog.Debugging.SelfLog.Enable(
    msg => System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine(msg));

//writes into Console window
Serilog.Debugging.SelfLog.Enable(System.Console.Error);

Please note that the internal logging will not write to other sinks.

Here is a more useful implementation where we write to a file based on a configuration enablement.

if (bool.TrueString == config["SelfLog:Enabled"])
{
   var path = Path.Combine(config["SelfLog:Path"], "log.log");

   if (File.Exists(path)) 
      path = Path.Combine(config["SelfLog:Path"], $"log-{Guid.NewGuid()}.log");

   var file = File.CreateText(path);
   Serilog.Debugging.SelfLog.Enable(TextWriter.Synchronized(file));
}

This is how I use in my Production environment. I just enable it when necessary.

💡Be very careful when writing to Files or Console Stream. It will slow down your application.


Customizing Output Formats

Serilog provides several built-in output formats, such as the default text format, JSON format, and XML format, which can be used to control the way log events are written to output sinks.

To configure the output format of Serilog, you can use the WriteTo method on the logger configuration object to define the custom formatter.

Here is an example of how to configure the output format of Serilog to JSON format:

Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .WriteTo.Console(new JsonFormatter())
    .CreateLogger();

In this example, we configure Serilog to write log events to the console sink using the JsonFormatter. The JsonFormatter formats the log events in JSON format, making it easier to integrate with other tools and services.


Customizing Output Templates

Output templates in Serilog define the structure and content of log events. They are used to format log messages with additional context information, such as the timestamp, log level, and other custom properties.

Here is a simple example:

//create the logger and setup your sinks, filters and properties
Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .WriteTo.Console(outputTemplate:        
    	"[{Timestamp:HH:mm:ss} {Level:u3}] {Message:lj}{NewLine}{Exception}"))
    .CreateBootstrapLogger();

The following fields can be used in a custom output template:

 * Exception: The exception associated with the log event, if any
 * Level: The log level of the event (e.g. Information, Warning, Error).
 * Message: The message of the log event
 * NewLine: Inserts a new line character in the output
 * SourceContext: The source context of the log event, usually the name of the class or component that generated the log message
 * Timestamp: The timestamp of the log event in the default format.

💡These fields are enabled by default. You can create a custom Enricher and add more fields (custom fields).


Set Up from JSON Configuration File

Serilog provides several ways to configure its settings, including through code, XML configuration files, and JSON configuration files. In this section, we will discuss how to configure Serilog using a JSON configuration file.

To read Serilog configuration from a JSON file, we need to add the Serilog.Settings.Configuration NuGet package to our project. This package contains a ReadFrom method that can read Serilog configuration settings from various sources, including JSON files.

dotnet add package Serilog.Settings.Configuration

Here's an example of how to read Serilog configuration from a JSON file:

using Serilog;
using Serilog.Settings.Configuration;

Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .ReadFrom.Configuration(new ConfigurationBuilder()
        .AddJsonFile("logging.json")
        .Build())
    .CreateLogger();

Here's an example of a JSON configuration file for Serilog:

{
  "Serilog": {
    "MinimumLevel": {
      "Default": "Information"
    },
    "WriteTo": [
      {
        "Name": "Console",
        "Args": {
          "outputTemplate": "[{Timestamp:HH:mm:ss} {Level:u3}] {Message:lj}{NewLine}{Exception}"
        }
      },
      {
        "Name": "File",
        "Args": {
          "path": "logs/log.txt",
          "rollingInterval": "Day",
          "outputTemplate": "[{Timestamp:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.fff zzz}] [{Level}] {Message}{NewLine}{Exception}"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}

By using a JSON configuration file, we can easily configure Serilog without having to modify our code. This makes it easier to change the logging configuration without having to rebuild and redeploy our application.

Here is similar to what I'm using today.

{
  "Serilog": {
    "Using": ["Serilog.Sinks.Console"],
    👇 Define custom levels per Source Context
    "MinimumLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Override": {
        "Microsoft": "Information",
        "System": "Information",
        "Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.Diagnostics": "Warning",
        "OpenIddict.Server.OpenIddictServerDispatcher": "Warning",
        "Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command": "Warning",
        "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"
      }
    },
    "WriteTo": [
      👇 My Sinks go here - check out the following sections
    ],
    👇 The Enrichers that I Love 💪
    "Enrich": [
      "WithAssemblyName",
      "WithEnvironmentUserName",
      "FromLogContext",
      "WithMachineName",
      "WithExceptionDetails"
    ],
    "Destructure": [
      {
        "Name": "ToMaximumDepth",
        "Args": { "maximumDestructuringDepth": 4 }
      },
      {
        "Name": "ToMaximumStringLength",
        "Args": { "maximumStringLength": 100 }
      },
      {
        "Name": "ToMaximumCollectionCount",
        "Args": { "maximumCollectionCount": 10 }
      }
    ],
    👇 Custom Properties to Identify what Application and Environment is this
    "Properties": {
      "Application": "Application 1",
      "Environment": "Production"
    }
  }
}



Writting to Sink File in Asynchronous manner

When writing logs to a file, we can improve the performance of our application by using an asynchronous file sink. An asynchronous file sink writes log events to a file in a separate thread, which allows the main thread of our application to continue without being blocked.

To implement this in our project, we need to install the Serilog.Sinks.Async package via NuGet.

dotnet add Serilog.Sinks.Async

Here is the final configuration file.

{
    "Serilog": {
        "MinimumLevel": {},
        "WriteTo": [
            {
                "Name": "Async",
                "Args": {
                    "configure": [
                        {
                            "Name": "File",
                            "Args": {
                                "Path": "D:/Logs/log.txt",
                                "rollingInterval": "Day",
                                "outputTemplate": "{Timestamp:HH:mm:ss} [{Level:u3}][{UserId}] [{CorrelationId}:{SourceContext}] {Message}{NewLine}{Exception}"
                            }
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        ],
        "Enrich": [],
        "Destructure": [],
        "Properties": {}
    }
}

Easy as is being shown here. You just need to wrap the Sink configuration inside the Async Sink.


Sink Logs to Elastic Search

In addition to writing logs to files and the console, Serilog also provides a sink for writing logs to Elasticsearch, Splunk, Seq, Application Insights, among others. This can be useful for centralized log management and analysis.

To write logs to Elasticsearch, we need to install the Serilog.Sinks.Elasticsearch package via NuGet.

dotnet add Serilog.Sinks.Elasticsearch

And we can then enable the Elasticsearch sink by adding the following configuration to our logging.json file:

{
    "Serilog": {
      "Using": [],
      "MinimumLevel": {},
      "WriteTo": [
        {
          "Name": "Elasticsearch",
          "Args": {
            "nodeUris": "https://elastic-node1.com/;https://elastic-node2.com",
            "connectionGlobalHeaders": "Authorization=Basic XXXXXXXXXXXXXX==",
            "indexFormat": "frontend-app-logs-{0:yyyy.MM}",
            "batchPostingLimit": 50,
            "batchAction": "Create",
            "restrictedToMinimumLevel": "Debug",
            "bufferBaseFilename": "D:/Logs/el-logs",
            "autoRegisterTemplate": true,

            👇 This enabled writing errors of the sink in the selflog sink
            "emitEventFailure": "WriteToSelfLog",

            👇 Should match the version of your Elastic Search
            "autoRegisterTemplateVersion": "ESv7",

            "registerTemplateFailure": "IndexAnyway",
            "inlineFields": true
          }
        }
      ],
      "Enrich": [],
      "Destructure": [],
      "Properties": {}
    }
  }

In this configuration, we use the WriteTo property to specify that we want to write logs to Elasticsearch. We specify the sink name as "Elasticsearch", and provide the configuration parameters as arguments in the Args property.

We also specify the format of the index name using the indexFormat parameter. In this example, we use "myapp-{0:yyyy.MM.dd}", which creates an index with a name that includes the current date in the format "myapp-yyyy.MM.dd". This can be useful for organizing logs by date.

Finally, we enable auto-registration of the Elasticsearch index template using the autoRegisterTemplate parameter. This creates a template that defines the mapping of log events to Elasticsearch fields.

Once we have added this configuration to our logging.json file, we are good to go.


Sink Logs to Application Insights

The Application Insights sink for Serilog allows you to easily write log events to Microsoft Azure's Application Insights service. This can be useful for monitoring and analyzing logs from distributed applications.

To use the Application Insights sink, you will first need to add the Serilog.Sinks.ApplicationInsights package to your project. You can do this using the Package Manager Console in Visual Studio by running the following command:

dotnet add Serilog.Sinks.ApplicationInsights

Once you have installed the package, you can add the Application Insights sink to your Serilog configuration. This can be done in your serilog.json configuration file as follows:

{
  "Serilog": {
    "WriteTo": [
      {
        "Name": "ApplicationInsights",
        "Args": {
          "instrumentationKey": "<your-instrumentation-key>"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}

Replace <your-instrumentation-key> with your Application Insights instrumentation key. You can find this key in the Azure portal under your Application Insights resource.








Some Common Sinks




Making your own Enricher

Check out this blog post 👉https://rmauro.dev/serilog-custom-enricher-on-aspnet-core/






More Resources about Serilog




