BenchmarkDotNet is a powerful library for benchmarking .NET code, targeting C# / VB.NET / F#. BenchmarkDotNet is capable of measure the performance of our code in many aspects, such as different .NET frameworks (.NET 6, .NET5, .NET 3.1, .NET Framework 4.8, etc), architectures (X64, x86, ARM), Operational Systems (such as Linux, Windows), among many others.
And many aspects of our code, such as Tread Allocation, Memory Allocations and more.

In other words to measure many aspects of a method/function using different configurations.

In this blog post let's go though the basic steps to have BenchmarkDotNet measuring our code performance.

In this post create our first benchmark test for manipulation of Strings

💡Spoiler Alert: What seems prettiest code sometimes it's not the fastest. You have to check and measure to be sure.


Here is a sample output - Benchmark over TimeAgo function in the next episode.


|                         Method |         Mean |       Error
|------------------------------- |-------------:|------------
|               BenchmarkTimeAgo |   1,667.9 ns |    19.43 ns
|  BenchmarkTimeAgoStackOverflow |     416.6 ns |     1.76 ns


💬In this Issue

 * Project Set up
 * Creating and Running the Tests
 * Reading the Results
 * Benchmark Diagnostics
 * Extra: Using Exporters


Project Set Up

Before getting started you need to have installed .NET6 SDK
https://dotnet.microsoft.com/en-us/download

To get started let's create our project using the empty console template.
Run the following code in your terminal.

# creates the solution
$ dotnet new sln -n BenchmarkStrings

# creates the project
$ dotnet new console -n BenchmarkStrings

# add the project to the solution
$ dotnet sln BenchmarkStrings.sln add BenchmarkStrings\BenchmarkStrings.csproj

# navigate to csproj folder
$ cd BenchmarkStrings

# adding the Benchmark package
$ dotnet add package BenchmarkDotNet

# an additional optional package that provides a set of Windows diagnosers
$ dotnet add package BenchmarkDotNet.Diagnostics.Windows

# creates global.json

$ dotnet new globaljson --sdk-version 6.0.100

We should end up with the following structure:


Creating the Tests

For our test case let's measure the speed and performance of the String Manipulations methods.

To create a Test is simple as declare a method and decorate with the attribute [Benchmark]. That is it.

Jumping to the code. Setup your tests as each individual method.

using BenchmarkDotNet.Attributes;

[Benchmark]
string StringConcatenation()
{
    string c = string.Empty;
    for(var i = 0; i < 1_000; i++)
    {
        c = c + i.ToString();
    }
    return c;
}


[Benchmark]
string StringFormatConcatenation()
{
    string c = string.Empty;
    for(var i = 0; i < 1_000; i++)
    {
        c = string.Format("{0}{1}", c, i);
    }
    return c;
}

[Benchmark]
string StringBuilderConcatenation()
{
    System.Text.StringBuilder sb = new System.Text.StringBuilder();
    for(var i = 0; i < 1_000; i++)
    {
        sb.Append(i);
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}

