{"version":"0.3.1","atoms":[],"cards":[["markdown",{"markdown":"In this blog post, we'll explore how to use the [HangFire](https://www.hangfire.io/) library to implement background job processing in .NET, specifically focusing on the Outbox pattern. By the end of this guide, you'll be able to set up a simple application that uses HangFire to manage recurring background jobs efficiently.

## Introduction to HangFire

HangFire is a powerful library for background job processing in .NET applications. It allows you to create, process, and monitor background jobs without the need for a separate background processing worker. Ideal for scenarios like sending emails or processing queued items, HangFire can be easily integrated into your existing applications.

### The Outbox Pattern Overview

The Outbox pattern is a design pattern that ensures messages are sent to a message broker after a successful database transaction. This pattern is particularly useful for reliable message delivery in distributed systems. By using the Outbox pattern, we can ensure that messages are only sent if they were successfully saved in the database.

![outbox-pattern.drawio](https://res-3.cloudinary.com/dgoa5llvh/image/upload/q_auto/outbox-pattern.drawio.png)

## Setting Up Your Project with HangFire

To demonstrate HangFire with the Outbox pattern, let's start by installing the necessary NuGet packages. Make sure you have the following packages installed in your .NET project:

```bash
dotnet add package Hangfire.AspNetCore
dotnet add package Hangfire.PostgreSql
```

Next, you need to configure HangFire in your `Startup.cs` or `Program.cs` (for .NET 6 and later).

### Configuring Dependency Injection

Here's how you can set up HangFire using Dependency Injection:

```csharp
public static IHostBuilder CreateHostBuilder(string[] args) =>
    Host.CreateDefaultBuilder(args)
        .ConfigureServices((hostContext, services) =>
        {
            services.AddHangfire(x => x.UsePostgreSqlStorage("YourConnectionStringHere"));
            services.AddHangfireServer();
        });
```

### Creating a Background Job

Now, let's create a background job that processes outbox messages. This job will check the database for any outbox messages, process them, and mark them as completed:

```csharp
public class ProcessOutboxMessagesJob
{
    private readonly IDatabaseService _databaseService;

    public ProcessOutboxMessagesJob(IDatabaseService databaseService)
    {
        _databaseService = databaseService;
    }

    public async Task ProcessAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        using var connection = _databaseService.GetConnection();
        using var transaction = await connection.BeginTransactionAsync(cancellationToken);

        var outboxMessages = await _databaseService.GetOutboxMessagesAsync(connection, transaction);

        if (!outboxMessages.Any()) return;

        foreach (var message in outboxMessages)
        {
            // Deserialization logic ... 
            // Publish message logic ...
            await _databaseService.UpdateOutboxMessageAsync(message, connection, transaction);
        }

        await transaction.CommitAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

## Scheduling the Background Job

Once you have defined your job, you can schedule it using HangFire's recurring job feature:

```csharp
public static class BackgroundJobExtensions
{
    public static void UseBackgroundJobs(this IApplicationBuilder app)
    {
        app.UseHangfireDashboard();
        app.UseHangfireServer();

        var recurringJobManager = app.ApplicationServices.GetService<IRecurringJobManager>();
        recurringJobManager.AddOrUpdate<ProcessOutboxMessagesJob>(
            "OutboxProcessor",
            j => j.ProcessAsync(CancellationToken.None),
            Cron.Secondly); // Every second for example
    }
}
```

### Handling Consecutive Executions

It's crucial to consider what happens if your job takes longer to complete than its scheduled frequency. Implementing row-level locking can prevent multiple instances of your job from processing the same messages at the same time:

```sql
SELECT * FROM OutboxMessages FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;
```

This SQL command will select rows in a way that locks them for processing while allowing other jobs to skip over locked messages, maintaining concurrency.

## Monitoring Jobs with HangFire Dashboard

HangFire comes equipped with a built-in dashboard to monitor your jobs. You can access it in your application at the `/hangfire` endpoint. This enables you to view active jobs, their statuses, and more.

## Conclusion

In this blog post, we've looked at how to integrate HangFire in a .NET application and implement the Outbox pattern for reliable message processing. With the combination of HangFire and a well-implemented Outbox pattern, you can ensure that messages are handled correctly even in the face of failures or scaling challenges.

If you'd like to learn more about HangFire or the Outbox pattern, consider diving into additional resources or watching related tutorials.

Happy coding!"}]],"markups":[],"sections":[[10,0],[1,"p",[]]],"ghostVersion":"4.0"}

