AI is transforming software development, and Semantic Kernel (SK) makes it easy to integrate AI models like OpenAI’s GPT and locally hosted models like Ollama.

Instead of manually handling API calls, SK provides a structured way to interact with AI in your applications.


Let's go though these topics

 * Set up both OpenAI and Ollama (local LLM) as AI models
 * Enable conversational AI for dynamic back-and-forth interactions
 * Add conversation history, so the AI can “remember” past messages

When completed we’ll have a command-line chatbot that can answer questions and summarize topics using either OpenAI or Ollama, with memory for context.


Setting Up the Project

Ensure you have:

 * .NET 6 or later
 * An OpenAI API key or Ollama installed

Create a New .NET Console Project using the following commands.

# creates a new project
dotnet new console -n SemanticKernelChat

cd SemanticKernelChat

# install depdencies
dotnet add package Microsoft.SemanticKernel
dotnet add package Spectre.Console


Let's use Spectre.Console for a simple command-line interface with colors and prompts.

In the Program.cs file add the following.

using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Connectors.OpenAI;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Connectors.Ollama;
using Spectre.Console;
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

var choice = AnsiConsole.Prompt(
    new SelectionPrompt<string>()
        .Title("Choose AI Provider:")
        .AddChoices("OpenAI (GPT-4)", "Ollama (Local)")
);

Kernel kernel = choice.Contains("OpenAI") ? SetupOpenAI() : SetupOllama();
List<string> conversationHistory = new();

AnsiConsole.MarkupLine("[bold green]AI Chatbot is ready. Type 'exit' to quit.[/]");

while (true)
{
    string userInput = AnsiConsole.Ask<string>("[yellow]You:[/] ");
    if (userInput.ToLower() == "exit") break;

    conversationHistory.Add($"User: {userInput}");

    AnsiConsole.Status().Start("Thinking...", async ctx =>
    {
        var chatFunction = kernel.CreateSemanticFunction(@"
            Maintain context across messages. Here is the conversation history:
            {{$history}}
            Respond to the latest user input:
            {{$input}}
        ");


        string history = string.Join("\n", conversationHistory);
        string response = await kernel.RunAsync(new() { { "history", history }, { "input", userInput } }, chatFunction);

        conversationHistory.Add($"AI: {response}");
        AnsiConsole.MarkupLine($"[bold cyan]AI:[/] {response}");
    });
}

AnsiConsole.MarkupLine("[bold red]Goodbye![/]");

//adds OpenAI ChatCompletion to the Kernel 
static Kernel SetupOpenAI()
  => new KernelBuilder()
      .WithOpenAIChatCompletion("gpt-4", "YOUR_OPENAI_API_KEY")
      .Build();

//adds Ollama ChatCompletion to the Kernel 
static Kernel SetupOllama()
  => new KernelBuilder()
      .WithOllamaChatCompletion("mistral")
      .Build();

Save your file and run.

> dotnet run

Sample Conversation with Memory
Choose AI Provider:
  1. OpenAI (GPT-4)
  2. Ollama (Local)

> OpenAI (GPT-4)

AI Chatbot is ready. Type 'exit' to quit.

You: What is the capital of France?
Thinking...
AI: The capital of France is Paris.

You: Who is the president there?
Thinking...
AI: The current president of France is Emmanuel Macron.

You: What is his political stance?
Thinking...
AI: Emmanuel Macron is a centrist politician, focusing on pro-European policies and economic reform.

You: Summarize our conversation.
Thinking...
AI: So far, we've discussed that the capital of France is Paris, the current president is Emmanuel Macron, and he is a centrist politician with pro-European policies.

You: exit

Goodbye!

We’ve successfully built an AI-powered chatbot with:

✅ Semantic Kernel for AI integration.

✅ OpenAI (Cloud) and Ollama (Local) models.

✅ A conversational loop for dynamic interactions.

✅ Conversation memory for better context retention.

